## Complete basic {{ provisioner_name }} setup for *head node*

At this point, all of the packages necessary to use {{ provisioner_name }} on
the *head node* should be installed. Next, we create and enable the Container
Registry and S3 Object store. These are optional if you have an existing S3 or
Container Registry available for use at your local site.

::: {.tip}
The S3 and registry containers are configured as [Podman
Quadlets](https://docs.podman.io/en/latest/markdown/podman-systemd.unit.5.html),
which interface with systemd allowing you to perform normal systemd operations
on them like:

```bash
systemctl status registry.service
systemctl restart seaweedfs.service
journalctl -u registry.service
```
:::

First create local directories for container storage.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Create directories for registry and S3 containers -->
```bash
mkdir -p /opt/ohpc/admin/data/oci
mkdir -p /opt/ohpc/admin/data/s3
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Now create the container registry quadlet definition.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Create container registry quadlet -->
```bash
cat > /etc/containers/systemd/registry.container <<EOF
# registry.container
[Unit]
Description=Image OCI Registry
After=network-online.target
Requires=network-online.target

[Container]
ContainerName=registry
HostName=registry
Image=docker.io/library/registry:latest
Volume=/opt/ohpc/admin/data/oci:/var/lib/registry:Z
PublishPort=5000:5000

[Service]
TimeoutStartSec=0
Restart=always

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Then create the local S3 object store quadlet definition. MinIO is no longer
usable here: current MinIO container images are no longer published under an
open source license. [SeaweedFS](https://github.com/seaweedfs/seaweedfs) is
used instead, since its S3 gateway is compatible enough with the S3 API that
none of the later `s3cmd` steps in this guide (bucket creation, ACLs, bucket
policies, anonymous object reads) need to change.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Create SeaweedFS S3 object storage quadlet -->
```bash
cat > /etc/containers/systemd/seaweedfs.container <<EOF
[Unit]
Description=SeaweedFS S3
After=local-fs.target network-online.target
Wants=local-fs.target network-online.target

[Container]
ContainerName=seaweedfs
Image=docker.io/chrislusf/seaweedfs:latest
# Volumes
Volume=/opt/ohpc/admin/data/s3:/data:Z

# Ports
PublishPort=9000:9000
PublishPort=9091:23646

# Environment Variables
Environment=AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=admin
Environment=AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=password1234

# Command to run in container
Exec=mini -dir=/data -s3.port=9000

[Service]
Restart=always

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

We need to reload the system daemon, and to allow the SeaweedFS, and OCI
Registry containers to start.

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Reload Systemd daemon and start the s3 (seaweedfs) and container registry -->
```bash
systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl start seaweedfs.service
systemctl start registry.service
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->

Configure ssh
<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Configure ssh -->
```bash
# Handle SSH to compute nodes
echo -e "Host ${compute_prefix}*\n\tStrictHostKeyChecking=no" \
	>> /etc/ssh/ssh_config.d/40-ohpc.conf
```
<!-- ohpc_end -->
